export default {"id":"docs-implementation-plan","path":"docs/implementation-plan.md","title":"Implementation and qualification plan","parts":[{"id":"part-1","title":"Implementation and qualification plan","html":"<h1 id=\"implementation-and-qualification-plan\">Implementation and qualification plan</h1>\n<p>The launch custody profile uses software KMS and age-key custody on Cloudflare\nor Netlify. A protected runtime wrapping key stays outside the database, and\neach profile has an independently random data key. Scoped authorization,\ntenant isolation and required audit apply. Runtime or shared-key compromise\ncan expose reachable profile keys; software custody does not provide independent\nHSM or non-exportability guarantees.</p>\n<p>Each deployment must verify provider plans, issuer metadata, entitlements and\nrequired custody. Additional services or upgrades require separate operator\napproval. Hosting forecasts exclude labor, CRM/payment fees and customer pricing.\nSynthetic frontend previews do not establish a connected backend or production\nservice. Implementation and qualification evidence must match the selected\nrelease and deployment.</p>\n<p>This is the durable engineering contract, not a completion report. Implementation and qualification use explicitly selected, isolated development environments; verify the actual executor and checkout for each session. Operators install on their own infrastructure and supply host-specific feedback; existing operational hosts and active recovery work are not changed by this implementation task.</p>\n<p>V1 is one complete product contract. Work packages describe implementation\ndependencies and parallel ownership; they do not define reduced releases or\ndefer a promised capability. Source checkpoints make changes reviewable. Fixes\nmove the implementation forward against the same acceptance requirements.\nA full-v1 claim requires the whole declared contract and its revision-bound\nevidence. The acceptance target is the complete agreed end state. Optional\nintegrations remain selectable per deployment; that selection does not remove\nan agreed product capability. Cost discipline, checkpoints and bounded tests\nsupport the same target. Fix forward against failures without reducing scope,\nretaining obsolete fallback implementations or weakening gates to accommodate\nstale records. Preserve required data protection, reconciliation and recovery.\nRecord actual scope, limitations, cost and receipts in the existing\nrelease/qualification records.</p>\n<h2 id=\"accepted-product-decisions\">Accepted product decisions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Public project <code>maldous/CircularQuay</code>; Apache-2.0; native <code>cq</code> and independent <code>cq-audit</code> commands.</li>\n<li>One Go module with idiomatic package/file/asset names; native runtime distribution without requiring a language runtime.</li>\n<li>Explicit read-through interoperability; one authority per value and exact source/destination identity selection.</li>\n<li>SOPS/age offline, optional OpenBao Transit online, explicitly authorized hybrid snapshots, and separately labeled native deployed snapshots.</li>\n<li>Native Linux/systemd, Windows, Termux, container, Windmill, Stokowski and cloud recipes/adapters, qualified individually.</li>\n<li>Optional local/hosted authenticated MCP with filtered metadata, registered actions and separate per-user/client/key raw-read permissions.</li>\n<li>Provider-independent identity authorization: use an existing OAuth authorization server directly when it supports a registered CircularQuay API, or a self-hosted server such as Keycloak. No particular identity product, hosted broker or paid plan is a runtime prerequisite.</li>\n<li>Named issuer trust and typed access-token profiles, configurable scope/role mappings, exact principal/client/resource associations and bounded lifetimes. Context names and permissions are operator-defined; development/production labels are examples only.</li>\n<li>Standard asymmetric client authentication and sender constraints where qualified: private-key JWT, DPoP and mutual TLS. Offline identity authorization requires an issuer-authorized artifact binding and an explicit clock/revocation trust contract; an ordinary login token or OAuth refresh permission does not establish it.</li>\n<li>No extra recurring paid provider dependency for the personal reference; free testing accounts authorized, no automatic paid conversion.</li>\n<li>Strict first-party quality with transparent upstream advisory/mitigation reporting.</li>\n<li>Product-wide <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-security-profiles&amp;part=part-1\">default and strict security profiles</a>,\nmandatory baseline safeguards in both, centrally enforced downgrade resistance\nand exact control-to-schema/code/test/deployment evidence.</li>\n<li>Official Go, TypeScript and Python SDKs share the supported CLI/MCP contract,\nexplicit credentials, cancellation, safe errors and cross-language conformance.</li>\n<li>Runtime OpenTelemetry metrics, traces and structured diagnostics are v1\ncapabilities, configured through protected policy with bounded export and no\nvalue-bearing telemetry. Required security audit remains independently durable.</li>\n<li>Policy-controlled release awareness supports interactive notices, explicit\nautomation, stable/RC channels, enterprise feeds and air-gapped minimum versions.</li>\n<li>Public README for motivation/features/user usage; <code>RELEASE.md</code> for maintainer/operator account, index, audit, deployment and post-installation directions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"managed-key-product-contract\">Managed-key product contract</h2>\n<p>The optional <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-1\">managed key service</a> associates a verified\nexisting identity with BOTH authorized KMS and age-key associations. The latest\nexplicit decision requires automatic provisioning of both on authorized enrollment,\nwithout offline-key download or hybrid activation. Scoped UI/API/CLI/MCP\nadministration manages allowed principals, clients, windows, modification, and\nrevocation. Reuse available paid Netlify/Cloudflare identity and management\ncapabilities first for the minimal hosted API and confirmed CLI/text flow.\nProduction custom key-specific UI/screens remain unapproved. A separate frontend\ncheckout may build synthetic/mock functional previews and loopback\nlocal-configuration simulations, but no real backend, provider/key/account\naccess, deployment, purchase or new email/onboarding suite is authorized. Key distribution is\nexplicitly distinct from protected local custody and remote unlocking. Bundle,\nrecipient, recovery, and authorization-domain isolation apply to every unlock\npath. Private keys, released data keys, and plaintext cannot be recalled;\nSOPS/age do not enforce expiry. Commercial packaging and financial scenarios\nare assumptions, not chosen prices, traction, or deployment authorization.</p>\n<p>Production custom administration UI/screens remain unapproved, not a current\nservice implementation workstream. Prior UI proposals are not proof of delivery.\nThe explicit <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-3&amp;anchor=explicitly-confirmed-ui-design-and-bounded-preview-work\">landing/client-SPA design</a>\nauthorizes a separate frontend checkout for synthetic/mock functional previews\nand loopback local-configuration simulations only. It does not authorize real\nbackend/provider/key/account access, production deployment, purchase or a new\nsuite. Reuse existing platform services and preserve CLI/text onboarding and\nproduct capabilities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"established-baseline-and-first-run-acceptance\">Established baseline and first-run acceptance</h3>\n<p>The latest authorized launch baseline includes hosted SOFTWARE cryptographic\noperations and authenticated age-private-key retrieval, with BOTH associations\nautomatically provisioned on authorized profile enrollment. This supersedes the\nearlier age-only baseline, with explicit window checks, usage classes and an\nopt-in subscription opportunity. It does not include enterprise config\norchestration or every optional integration by default. Preserve those as separately selected\nCircularQuay capabilities and independently costed deployments. <strong>KMS</strong> is\nconfigured per user profile. Implement the\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-1&amp;anchor=per-profile-age-and-kms-custody\">separate custody contract</a>;\ncurrent source does not yet establish cloud-KMS support.</p>\n<p>First run resolves existing configuration, discovery or manual fallback,\nauthenticates through the selected identity, then immediately initializes the\nhosted key service unless explicitly disabled by CLI or configuration. Implement\nboth opt-out surfaces and deterministic precedence/lock-conflict handling\nbefore any hosted-service network call. Test default initialization, CLI\ndisable, config disable, conflicting/locked settings, untrusted/invalid source,\nself-hosted/offline paths and no personal-file/value/key uploads. Registration\nmust be authorized, idempotent and concurrency-safe for user/client/tenant and\nnoninteractive workload-identity CI enrollment; a retry creates no duplicate\nassociation or billing event.</p>\n<p>Managed release windows default on for future key retrieval; durations remain\npolicy-defined. Registration cannot grant an unauthorized key/tenant role or\nproduction access. Ending a release window does not expire age or revoke copied\nprivate keys/ciphertext. The hosted-access-event metric is defined; allowances,\nthresholds, prices and payment/hosting vendors are undecided; test usage notices and explicit subscription opportunity\nwithout automatic charging. Evaluate vendors against the existing baseline\nforecast without provisioning accounts or enrolling trials/purchases.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-5&amp;anchor=profile-utilization-and-fair-metering\">Profile utilization</a>\ncounts hosted access events, not profile count/key slots: one daily hosted fetch\nis one use, twenty independent hosts/fetches are twenty, local reuse adds zero.\nTest denied/precommit failures at zero customer uses, postcommit uncertain\nrelease reconciled once, and same-logical-access retries/concurrent requests\nwithout duplicate metering. Distinct hosts cannot share one idempotency identity\nto bypass independent access accounting. Provider attempts/logs/storage still\nincur hosting costs. The current forecast is HOSTING ONLY for Personal/Business/\nEnterprise product workloads, with total allocated versus already-paid incremental\nspend and allowance/DB-activity inflections. No labor, external KMS, CRM/payment\nfees, customer prices or margins are embedded; these examples authorize no spend.</p>\n<h3 id=\"per-profile-kms-and-hybrid-acceptance\">Per-profile KMS and hybrid acceptance</h3>\n<p>Implement <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-1&amp;anchor=per-profile-age-and-kms-custody\">custody selection</a>\nin the user profile: local age, hosted age-private-key retrieval, or a qualified\nremote KMS provider/key/auth identity with explicit scoped operation grants.\nThe current SOPS adapter rejects cloud-KMS/key-group metadata; reuse upstream\nbackends with constrained credentials rather than relabeling existing support.\nRequire common schema/validation and consistent CLI/API/SDK/MCP behavior;\nintended client UI parity remains design only. Do not invent deployed support,\nprovider permissions, flags, tools or provisioning authority.</p>\n<p>Keep custody distinct from availability mode. Online-only required KMS has no\noffline bypass. Explicit hybrid retains approved complete encrypted snapshot\nfallback on qualifying availability failure within its window, with existing\nscope/freshness/audit/fence checks. Denial, TLS/trust failure, malformed response\nor expired fallback cannot trigger downgrade. Test both allowed hybrid fallback\nand each prohibited downgrade. Offline remains explicitly local. Ordinary SOPS\nrecipients are alternatives, not AND; quorum needs an actually supported and\ntested group/threshold mechanism. Retained data keys/plaintext or age fallback\ncannot be remotely revoked or made to require KMS on every future decrypt.</p>\n<p>Implement dual-association enrollment with durable idempotency, partial-failure\nreconciliation, resource budgets and readiness only after both associations\nexist. Resolve hosted disable/locks before network. Association creation does\nnot release offline keys, activate hybrid or silently substitute age for\nrequired online KMS. No live paid provisioning is authorized here. Add actual per-provider operations/key-version/\naudit/recovery costs to the existing forecast before commercial commitment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"local-to-managed-migration-and-existing-assurance\">Local-to-managed migration and existing assurance</h3>\n<p>Implement the <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-2&amp;anchor=end-to-end-local-to-managed-migration\">end-to-end migration contract</a>,\nnot just a mode flag. Client-local metadata inventory/reconciliation covers age,\nOS/hardware/self-hosted KMS definitions, encrypted assets, recipient/key versions,\nprofile grants/windows and recovery dependencies. Distinguish authorized reuse,\nexport/import, compatible data-key rewrap and payload re-encryption. Non-exportable\nhardware keys cannot be copied; actual provider formats/import permissions and\nsource unwrap authority must be verified. Preserve originals/provenance and\nseparate local-key import from automatic dual association creation.</p>\n<p>Use a scoped approved plan, staged protected output, durable migration IDs and\nper-asset reconciliation; interruption/retry cannot orphan duplicate paid keys\nor activate a partial estate. Verify destination decrypt/content/recipient/key\nversion and recovery before atomic activation. Retirement and rollback need\ncurrent separate authority, with deny/replay generations preserved. Online-only\nhas no offline bypass; only explicit authorized hybrid can activate compatible\nfallback under existing availability/window/freshness/audit rules. Old ciphertext,\nprivate keys/data keys/plaintext remain usable by holders after migration.</p>\n<p>Apply existing SECURITY/threat-model/administration/security-profile/assurance\ncontrols, positive and adversarial tests and exact qualification evidence.\nReal-backend/native custody/migration/recovery evidence is separate from mocks;\nan unavailable authorized backend is unqualified. The explicitly authorized\nlaunch SOFTWARE custody tradeoff permits runtime-accessible protected KEKs and\nrandom profile DEKs, with shared-runtime/KEK blast radius disclosed; it does not\nsatisfy a stronger independent/HSM requirement. Auth, tenant/purpose scope, audit,\nreplay/fallback controls and first-party gates remain mandatory. The\nrevised <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-5&amp;anchor=hosting-economics-and-capacity-assumptions\">full dual-service model</a>\nis now hosting-only: actual native meter rates, explicit workload/free-owner/\nDB-active assumptions, allocated versus incremental spend and inflection points.\nLabor, paid external KMS, CRM/payment costs and customer margins are removed. No live key\ninventory/upload/migration, paid provisioning or new account connection is\nauthorized by this implementation plan.</p>\n<h3 id=\"client-only-configuration-acceptance\">Client-only configuration acceptance</h3>\n<p>All config content, source credentials, resolution/validation/explain and save\nare client-side. Browser/native clients access sources over the user&#x27;s own\nnetwork/VPN and credential boundary. Test that no content/credentials/provenance\nleak through hosted backend/proxy/analytics/logs, including corporate sources.\nBrowser CORS/file restrictions require honest permitted native CLI/SDK/local\nintegration, never a server proxy or silent unsupported options. Save locally.\nHosted state includes authenticated profile→KMS/age associations, crypto operations\nand necessary enrollment/window/metering/billing. Protect keys without display\nor logging. CLI/config disable before network supports fully client-only use.\nPer-profile KMS is a required capability,\nnot an existing enum/flag or permission for live key provisioning here.\nQualify compatible KMS/age ciphertext, recipient/key versions and independent\nkey IDs/windows for separately protected domains before hybrid activation.\nThis is design; production custom UI remains unapproved. Separately authorized\nsynthetic/mock previews and loopback local-configuration simulations do not\nchange the client-only or no-backend boundary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"complete-product-deployment-and-cost-discipline\">Complete-product deployment and cost discipline</h3>\n<p>Minimize Cloudflare/Netlify spend using actual tiers/limits and qualified\ncontrols that satisfy the complete agreed deployment requirements. A paid\nproduct name is not a mandatory dependency when an equivalent qualified\nmechanism satisfies the policy. Cost limits cannot silently drop agreed\ncapabilities, relax a strict requirement or redefine release acceptance.\nKeep authentication, tenant isolation, provider plus application-level\nkey encryption/custody, durable event metering and sensible abuse controls.\nThe current hosting-only forecast distinguishes product workloads from provider\nplans, already-paid incremental spend and measured allowance/DB-activity limits.\nPaid external KMS/IdP/monitor/helpdesk/upgrades are not launch prerequisites.\nActual tier/project/qualified customer identity metadata is unverified, so choose\nCF first only if those existing facts qualify it; otherwise evaluate the existing\nNetlify included-Identity branch without purchasing or connecting accounts.\nProduction custom UI remains outside deployment authorization; authorized\nisolated synthetic frontend development continues. All config stays client-side,\nand no lower-priced plan inherits a higher-tier\nentitlement or an unmeasured reliability claim.</p>\n<h3 id=\"managed-key-service-production-acceptance\">Managed key-service production acceptance</h3>\n<p>The managed key service must satisfy <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-5&amp;anchor=production-protection-and-service-objectives\">production protection and service objectives</a>\nwithout becoming a config/backend orchestration service. Qualify exact WAF/bot/\nDDoS and per-IP/client/profile/tenant limit entitlements, headless CLI/CI access,\nauthoritative revocation/idempotency/usage consistency, encrypted durable key\nstorage/custody/residency, independent backup/restore/rotation and secret-free\nobservability with no public key-response caching. Measure realistic\navailability/latency and geography/failure cases; no platform-name or free-tier\nclaim substitutes for evidence. Price fixed and usage-dependent protection,\nlimiter, storage, authentication, retention and operational costs before a plan\nis called profitable. The guide&#x27;s SLOs/plan proposals are design/sensitivity\ninputs, not achieved results, live tariffs or procurement permission.</p>\n<h3 id=\"end-customer-account-service-acceptance\">End-customer account service acceptance</h3>\n<p><a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-4&amp;anchor=end-customer-accounts-subscriptions-and-support\">Account/subscription design</a>\nincludes authenticated profile-successful-fetch usage/history, invoices,\nplans/quotas/status, support and customer lifecycle. Qualify actual customer\nidentity, provider-hosted payment/subscription portals and support services\nseparately from Cloudflare/Netlify operator dashboards. No provider-admin\ncredentials or payment-card custody enter the client/model. Configuration and source credentials remain entirely local; synthetic/mock\naccount previews may be developed separately, while production account UI\nremains unapproved.</p>\n<p>Relevant CLI/API/SDK/MCP operations require common scopes, field/tenant isolation,\nidempotent meter/payment/subscription reconciliation, signed-event verification,\ndurable audit and safe operation state. Future unattended financial execution\nrequires an explicit user-approved action/account/amount/currency/plan/time\nboundary and applicable confirmation; capability does not authorize charges.\nTest wrong-customer IDs, stale/duplicate/out-of-order events, lost responses,\npayment/portal/support denial, budget/approval expiry and all cross-tenant paths.\nPrice support seats/labor, account history/storage/traffic and provider Billing\nfees in the existing forecast without buying or connecting any service.</p>\n<h3 id=\"restricted-operator-administration-design\">Restricted operator administration design</h3>\n<p>The <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-5&amp;anchor=restricted-remotely-hosted-operator-administration\">operator plane</a>\nis separate from customer billing and client-config SPA. It handles scoped\ncustomer/key metadata (public recipient/fingerprint only), activity/security/\nusage and coarse geographic context, safe database/support/monitoring and\nmaintenance. Strong operator auth, distinct privileges, bounded/audited reads\nand mutations, key redaction, privacy/retention and exact provider entitlements\nare required. No client config/source credentials or routine private-key display.\nReuse hosting/payment/support/monitoring consoles. Production custom UI remains\noutside deployment authorization; the isolated synthetic preview has its own\nexplicit scope. ISO27001 alignment is an evidence goal, not certification. No live\ncustomer data or maintenance action is authorized by design.</p>\n<h3 id=\"account-rbac-and-profile-scoped-enterprise-operations\">Account RBAC and profile-scoped enterprise operations</h3>\n<p><a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-4&amp;anchor=account-rbac-and-scoped-authority\">Account RBAC</a> separates\npermissions from tuple-bound tenant/org/customer/group/environment/fleet/\nkey-metadata/billing scope. Exactly one protected global application superuser\nis required by design, not ordinary support and not an implicit key-reader.\nUse strong auth, scoped routine identities, audited use and controlled recovery\nthat never leaves two active roots. Individual support assignments cover only\nspecified enterprise/customer subsets; agents have distinct bounded identities.\nTest deny/conflict/time/delegation/revocation and cross-assignment scope-mixing\nacross UI/API/CLI/SDK/MCP, including financial confirmation and key-custody denial.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-4&amp;anchor=customer-enterprise-administration-and-selected-fleet\">Customer enterprise/fleet design</a>\nis separate from provider support. Verify actual IdP/API scopes, browser/VPN/\nCORS/file access and explicit selected-agent capability. All config/source\ncredentials stay client-side. If a central fleet broker or provider function\nrequires hosted config/credential storage, report the conflict/options rather\nthan relax the boundary. No fleet agent/control command is verified in this\nsource checkpoint; production custom-console deployment remains unapproved.\nThe agreed product scope and authorized synthetic preview remain intact. Price\noperator/SSO/audit/recovery and optional fleet/provider costs independently;\nno live accounts, grants, fleet actions or infrastructure changes are authorized.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-2","title":"Managed-service development and delivery contract","html":"<h2 id=\"managed-service-development-and-delivery-contract\">Managed-service development and delivery contract</h2>\n<p>The managed service is developed as the same versioned artifact and migration\nsequence used for controlled releases. Its local developer deployment is\nloopback-isolated, carries its own tenant/domain, storage, certificate authority,\nand synthetic fixtures, and exercises verified authentication and server-side\nauthorization. A local origin, test header, developer account, or environment\nlabel is never an authorization shortcut. Keycloak, wire-compatible identity,\nand selected OpenBao fixtures are optional protocol inputs with fabricated\nidentities and secrets; they do not define a required customer IdP or an\nimplicit production trust relationship.</p>\n<p>Per-instance CA trust is isolated to the test clients and removed with the\nfixture. No permanent trust-store modification or TLS bypass is acceptable.\nLocal and CI runs share pinned artifacts, policy fixtures, migrations, health\nchecks, cleanup rules, and redacted evidence. Parallel runs receive separate\ntenant, storage, audit, and synthetic identity namespaces. Netlify Dev and\nWrangler adapter tests use that same authenticated contract, while preview\nvalidation is independently scoped to a preview application, audience, storage,\nand synthetic keys. It never has production mapping or custody access.</p>\n<p>Before promotion, managed-service qualification covers authentication/audience\nbinding, tenant/domain isolation, scopes, optimistic mapping updates, expiry,\nrevocation, concurrent changes, backup/restore, migration/rollback, health,\nand durable redacted audit. Provider integration, native runtime, preview, and\nproduction receipts remain separate. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-3&amp;anchor=local-development-integration-and-controlled-delivery\">SPIFFE/SPIRE</a>\ncan complement an IdP with workload mTLS identity, but each trust domain,\nattestation, selector, and policy association is explicit; workload identity\ndoes not by itself grant tenant, mapping, or key access.</p>\n<p>Release delivery follows the Gitflow contract: feature branches integrate into\n<code>develop</code>, a release branch stabilizes v1 release candidates, and approved\nstable artifacts reach <code>main</code>. Every release candidate and stable artifact is\nimmutable, versioned, digest-identified, and qualified as its own bytes.\nRebuilds, rebranding, changed build inputs, and changed migrations create a new\ncandidate and repeat the applicable gates. Production promotion uses the exact\ntested artifact and approved environment only after migration/rollback, health,\nauthorization isolation, backup/restore, and audit evidence pass. Publisher,\naccount, registry, and operator procedures remain in <a href=\"/explore#doc=release&amp;part=part-1\">RELEASE.md</a>.\nThe <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-qualification&amp;part=part-6&amp;anchor=managed-service-design-status\">qualification matrix</a>\nrecords the actual managed-service implementation and qualification status.</p>\n<h2 id=\"non-negotiable-boundaries\">Non-negotiable boundaries</h2>\n<p>The complete v1 <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-management-and-discovery&amp;part=part-1\">discovery and management contract</a>\nincludes typed remote enrollment, tenant/principal/client/provider resources,\nconfiguration revisions, scoped grants, delegation, approvals, revocation,\naudit and recovery. Separate runtime-policy, client-enrollment and managed\ndeployment schemas prevent local CRUD from becoming implicit remote authority.\nThe API, official Go/TypeScript/Python SDKs, CLI/UI and optional management MCP\nshare one operation map. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-administration-security&amp;part=part-1\">Administration security</a>\ndefines mandatory signed intent/publication, independent approval, trust/key\nlifecycle, freshness, replay/rollback, tenant isolation and durable mutation.</p>\n<p>Do not implement new cryptography, a vault, arbitrary expression evaluator, global credential broker, universal process supervisor or general lease-renewal daemon. Reuse upstream SOPS, native provider SDK/auth lifecycle and platform facilities.</p>\n<p>Do not access existing operational values to populate this repository. No live secret, private recipient, token, native credential blob or operational ciphertext belongs in tracked source, assets, fixture reports or public release metadata.</p>\n<p>No automatic history rewrite, estate-wide rotation or production service restart. A later approved migration must have explicit scope, one authoritative writer and tested rollback.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-packages-and-dependencies\">Work packages and dependencies</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Package</th>\n<th>Dependencies</th>\n<th>Acceptance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Foundation</td>\n<td>Execution authorization</td>\n<td>Canonical layout, model/schema, language/security ADR, synthetic examples and required metadata.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy/core</td>\n<td>Foundation</td>\n<td>Pure decisions, exact scopes, collision checks, complete generations and no value-bearing diagnostics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offline/SOPS</td>\n<td>Policy/core</td>\n<td>Restricted subprocess, authenticated inputs, explicit recipients/endpoint allowlist and safe encrypted snapshots.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OpenBao reference</td>\n<td>Core and approved isolated capacity</td>\n<td>Transit/KV, native service auth, audit/seal bootstrap, denial and independent restore.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Providers</td>\n<td>Core</td>\n<td>Narrow value adapters with identity, version, lifecycle, typed failure and export contracts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Native delivery</td>\n<td>Core</td>\n<td>Protected process/file/native projections, correct restart/recreation behavior and scoped ownership.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MCP</td>\n<td>Core/provider boundaries</td>\n<td>Per-request verified identity, filtered discovery, action/read separation and safe output/audit behavior.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Official SDKs</td>\n<td>Stable CLI/MCP contracts</td>\n<td>Go, TypeScript and Python parity, typed safe errors, explicit disclosure, cancellation/reaping, native conformance, locked builds, registry install and provenance receipts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observability and operations</td>\n<td>Core/runtime boundaries</td>\n<td>Safe correlated metrics/traces/logs, finite queues/export/shutdown, collector failure isolation, operational health, update awareness and local version policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed hosted connections</td>\n<td>Qualified MCP and explicit hosted scope</td>\n<td>External IdP/resource-server separation; typed tenant/environment/principal/connection isolation; approved identity associations; client and operation grants; lifecycle/revocation bounds and administrative audit. Local/CI/preview/production use the controlled-delivery contract in <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-managed-key-service&amp;part=part-1\">managed key service</a>; see <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-hosted-identity&amp;part=part-1\">hosted identity</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administration and assurance profiles</td>\n<td>Core trust and managed-service contracts</td>\n<td>Authoritative v1 API/resource/error/event and enrollment/deployment schemas; signed configuration/change integrity; complete typed CLI/UI/SDK/MCP lifecycle; monotonic profile enforcement; approval/revocation/audit/HA and downgrade-negative evidence under <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-management-and-discovery&amp;part=part-1\">management</a>, <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-administration-security&amp;part=part-1\">administration security</a> and <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-security-profiles&amp;part=part-1\">security profiles</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assurance</td>\n<td>Starts with foundation</td>\n<td>Fresh independent Sonar route, scope/coverage validation, complementary checks, formal/conformance and negative tests.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution</td>\n<td>Tested artifacts</td>\n<td>Immutable native/Go/OCI/MCP/MCPB metadata, signatures, provenance, free account directions and clean install.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operator qualification</td>\n<td>Relevant implementations</td>\n<td>Real reboot/device/provider/client/restore receipts, not merely compilation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Independent packages can proceed in parallel after their contracts are fixed.\nIdentity interoperability is qualified by capability and configured token\nprofile, not by a provider&#x27;s support for login alone. A resource server accepts\naccess tokens for its own audience, never an ID token as a bearer substitute.\nProvider-specific claims such as delegated scopes, application roles and client\nidentity require explicit typed validation and mapping before they can grant\nan operation. The v1 JSON schema, Go model, validator, runtime, negative tests\nand operator reference change together; no accepted field may be ignored or\nclaim support for an unimplemented integration. External provider entitlements\nand free-tier limits remain deployment prerequisites, not CircularQuay gates\nthat can be bypassed by broadening trust.</p>\n<p>The initial public product and policy contract are v1. Release stabilization\nuses <code>release/1.0.0</code> and <code>v1.0.0-rc.1</code> candidates before a qualified <code>v1.0.0</code>.\nAn optional channel is advertised only for its implemented, qualified scope;\nits absence does not imply that an incomplete promised v1 capability is ready.</p>\n<p>Capability reviews distinguish value storage, bootstrap custody, human login,\nmachine identity, non-exportable key operations, application authorization and\nnative delivery. A connector name does not establish all of these capabilities.\nHost keychains and biometric/mobile approvals are separate integrations from\nprotected filesystem or systemd/DPAPI projections; their actual boundaries are\nrecorded in <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-credential-boundaries&amp;part=part-1\">credential boundaries</a>. Interactive\nuser-presence requirements cannot silently replace unattended recovery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"interface-and-data-contract\">Interface and data contract</h2>\n<p>Policy version 1 declares sources, workloads, bindings, targets, snapshot settings, grants and optional MCP. The schema has one canonical source under its owning package. Unknown fields, duplicate keys and invalid combinations fail validation.</p>\n<p>Each source is explicitly bound to a provider and allowed resource scope. Production authentication cannot quietly fall back to a broad interactive identity. Retrieved values are strings with source/version/lifecycle metadata; no recursive evaluation. Field selection is delivery minimization, not a claim of field-level upstream authorization.</p>\n<p>Resolve every required binding before activation. Track declaration/accepted, resolved, projected and active state separately. Independent provider values have no global transaction. No source error silently deletes destinations or overwrites native rotation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"startup-update-and-failure-behavior\">Startup, update and failure behavior</h2>\n<p>Online requires fresh retrieval at every launch, including crash restart. Offline/hybrid retain approved static last-known-good material during qualifying availability failures without requiring a human login. Denial, TLS/trust failure, malformed response and expiry never authorize fallback. Existing running processes retain their delivered values.</p>\n<p>Use native scheduling, with five-minute jittered local/Git/OpenBao defaults and provider-specific budgets/backoff. Warn after three failed scheduled checks and escalate after 24 hours without successful authenticated checking. Measure failed synchronization time, not the age of unchanged values.</p>\n<p>Native encrypted systemd/DPAPI projections are machine-local convenience, not canonical portable backup. Termux is post-first-unlock and best effort under Android background restrictions. Dynamic credentials do not become perpetual snapshots. Snapshot/export permissions are distinct from reads.</p>\n<h2 id=\"security-assurance\">Security assurance</h2>\n<p>Target zero first-party compiler/linter/Sonar issues and analysis warnings/errors. Cover overall and new code; no ignored/suppressed status used to manufacture zero. Require review evidence for actual hotspots. Complexity-rule limits replace the meaningless requirement of zero executable complexity.</p>\n<p>Target 100% coverable owned executable coverage, including native code in its appropriate lane. Publish denominator, missing instrumentation, per-platform results and legitimate aggregate. Add policy decisions, mutation/property/fuzz, fault and model-conformance evidence; execution coverage alone is not proof.</p>\n<p>Pin build/scanner/analyzer/model tools. Use Go vet/Staticcheck/gosec/govulncheck, CodeQL where applicable, installer/workflow checks, SBOM/advisory/license analysis, source secret prevention, archive/image inspection and real integration tests. Preserve every upstream advisory and tested mitigation. Block unmitigated exploitable risk.</p>\n<p>Model exact authorization, generation/fallback, expiry/rollback, bootstrap acyclicity and MCP disclosure/ownership using TLA+/TLC finite bounds. Publish assumptions and replay traces against code. No end-to-end formal-security or certification claim.</p>\n<h2 id=\"acceptance-scenarios\">Acceptance scenarios</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Clean install with minimal PATH, no language runtime and no network for an offline bundle.</li>\n<li>Exact allowed resource works; wrong account/tenant/project/resource fails before upstream value release.</li>\n<li>Conflicting names, forbidden variables, malicious references, oversized/unsupported values and hostile endpoints fail safely.</li>\n<li>Partial source/update/write/restart failures preserve a controlled complete prior generation.</li>\n<li>Online crash restart generates a fresh source retrieval; authorized static hybrid fallback works only for availability failures.</li>\n<li>Expired/leased/revoked values are not reactivated through cache or publication.</li>\n<li>Fabricated markers stay out of toolkit logs/argv/artifacts, except an intentionally authorized MCP read response.</li>\n<li>Linux/Windows no-login cold boot; Android&#x27;s expected first-unlock limit and actual background behavior recorded.</li>\n<li>Synthetic two-source worker resumes automatically after reconnect, rotation and reboot; isolated Windmill schedule variant.</li>\n<li>MCP wrong issuer/audience/client, guessed IDs, cross-user access, bad Host/Origin and mutable action bypass fail.</li>\n<li>Independent backup restoration works without old machine TPM/DPAPI state; OpenBao needs independent data/seal recovery.</li>\n<li>Unfamiliar developer follows only the tagged source and <code>RELEASE.md</code> to install, audit, provision a free synthetic provider, enroll, distribute or identify the exact remaining step, and clean up.</li>\n<li>The same allowed/denied/expired/cancelled operation has matching SDK semantics\nin Go, TypeScript and Python; no value enters argv, logs, error details or hooks.</li>\n<li>Synthetic OTLP collectors receive only the declared safe vocabulary. Exporter\noutage, queue saturation, cancellation and shutdown stay bounded and cannot\nwaive required audit or turn a completed effect into an automatic retry.</li>\n<li>Interactive release notices never consume workload input; automation has no\nprompt. Disabled/offline checks make no connection, while an explicit local\nminimum version denies sensitive operations before credential or provider use.</li>\n<li>SDK and native RC/stable artifacts have their own immutable versions and\nindependently verified registry/download, installation and provenance receipts.</li>\n<li>Enterprise access reviews, lifecycle/revocation, audit retention/export,\ntenant isolation and recovery satisfy the <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-enterprise-integration&amp;part=part-1\">enterprise contract</a>,\nwith clear customer responsibilities and no certification claim.</li>\n<li>Signed administrative intent, exact candidate/target/revision and independent\napproval survive retries/concurrency; signature confusion, wrong signers,\nreplay/rollback/freeze, tenant crossover and authority-change races deny.</li>\n<li>Default operation is quiet and deterministic without bypassing any baseline\ncontrol. Strict requirements propagate through every interface, descendant,\ncache and recovery path; unsupported assurance and attempted downgrade deny.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"canonical-configuration-and-lifecycle-acceptance\">Canonical configuration and lifecycle acceptance</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-configuration-governance&amp;part=part-1\">configuration-governance contract</a> and\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-credential-lifecycle&amp;part=part-1\">lifecycle contract</a> are part of the complete v1\nacceptance scope. Implement one typed policy graph with defaults, inherited\nlocks, deterministic group resolution, explicit override bounds and shared\nCLI/API/UI/SDK/MCP authorization, validation, revision/approval and required audit.\nNo interface-specific policy file or local edit can bypass the effective ceiling.</p>\n<p>Qualify optional SCIM provisioning/deprovisioning, reconciliation and stale-event\nfencing; supported provider-native rotation/revocation with exact version binding,\npartial/uncertain-effect recovery and old-version containment; GitHub scanning\nand optional GitGuardian block/warn/authorized-exception decisions with separate\npermissions, bounded metadata and freshness rules. Do not derive GitGuardian\nidentifiers from local secret hashes. Exposure, scanner closure, issuer\nrevocation and verified rotation have independent evidence. Unsupported provider\nmutations remain explicit failures with a scoped owner next step.</p>\n<p>Qualify optional workload OIDC/SPIFFE, device-posture/Conditional Access and\nexternal policy-engine profiles against the same canonical authority ceiling.\nRequired stale/unknown/undefined decisions deny, including OPA HTTP success\nwithout a typed allow. Complete versioned schema/data/API migrations preserve\nlocks, revocation/exposure history, replay and audit state; fix forward through\nreviewed corrected revisions, never revive a permissive old policy or call\npartial interface support the finished product.</p>\n<p>Test secret-free lifecycle/compromise outcomes through OTLP and SDK observers;\nexport neither guarantees immutable audit nor waives durable required audit.\nAccount connections, purchases, secret transmission and live issuer mutation\nneed independent authorization and qualified capability evidence. Mock fixtures,\ndocumentation and schema work do not establish any of those outcomes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"single-file-and-explicitly-composed-configuration-acceptance\">Single-file and explicitly composed configuration acceptance</h3>\n<p>Both self-contained policy files and master policies with explicitly trusted\nexternal department/local includes are required. They resolve to one common\nvalidated contract across CLI/API/SDK/MCP and managed-service boundaries; no\nsingle-physical-file restriction replaces that requirement. Authenticate each\nsource and version/digest pin, constrain namespace/ownership and transitive\nfetch authority, and enforce deterministic typed precedence/conflicts and\nmulti-membership without weakening enterprise locks or approved override limits.</p>\n<p>Test cycles, duplicate/aliased sources and object IDs, traversal/link escape,\nbounded loading, pin/trust drift, stale/unavailable inputs, and complete graph\nvalidation before atomic activation. Safe explain/provenance must enforce domain\nand viewer permissions, not expose personal content or credential references.\nThe corporate GitHub + protected personal local acceptance case must prove\npersonal secrets/keys never upload to corporate services, keys/grants remain\nseparate, and personal data cannot override corporate locks or infer production\naccess. Explicit refresh/trust and optional finite offline-cache policy preserve\nknown denies and govern both new activation and existing-generation validity.\nRequired migrations fix forward without dropping single-file compatibility.\nImplementation tests and revision-bound receipts remain distinct from this design.</p>\n<h2 id=\"self-service-help-and-documentation-acceptance\">Self-service help and documentation acceptance</h2>\n<p>Searchable help is part of the complete v1 product. Every rendered control and\nfeature has an indexed explanation, and each supported audience has complete\ntask journeys with prerequisites, permission boundaries, safe examples, expected\nresults and recovery or escalation steps. This includes local users, developers,\nsecurity and tenant administrators, operators, support engineers, maintainers\nand AI-agent integrators. A search box alone does not satisfy this contract.</p>\n<p>The manual joins actual CLI/configuration CRUD, Go/TypeScript/Python SDK examples,\nMCP-agent workflows and authoritative API/schema specifications. It reuses\ncanonical definitions instead of inventing commands, endpoints or supported\ncapabilities. Each topic binds feature/control identifiers, audience, journey,\ncontent, stable search/deep-link route, exact source version, owner and evidence.\nImplemented native behavior, synthetic interface demonstrations and intended\nservice capabilities remain distinguishable in revision-scoped evidence.</p>\n<p>Acceptance includes keyboard and assistive-technology behavior, readable mobile\nand desktop layouts, query/result/empty/error states, meaningful relevance tests,\ncomplete control/topic coverage, safe troubleshooting, installation/upgrade and\nrelease-verification journeys. Source and content owners verify accuracy, broken\nlinks, capability drift and index regeneration for each release. Search indexes\nonly approved static product documentation and control metadata; user policies,\nsecret values, protected references, configuration provenance, private evidence\nand queries do not enter hosted search, analytics or diagnostic logs.</p>\n<p>Retrieval and ranking run entirely in the client, without a remote search API\nor server-search dependency. Progressively load a versioned public static index\nand lazily load/cache article chunks while preserving complete searchable corpus\ncoverage. A titles-only fallback cannot stand in for content search. Verify cold\nand warm transfer size/latency, repeated-query throughput and editing/input\nresponsiveness on the supported desktop/mobile routes. Static public content\ndelivery is distinct from transmitting a user&#x27;s search query to a service.</p>\n<p>The client binds the reviewed help distribution root and expected content\nversion before accepting a manifest or its article graph. Per-article hashes\ncannot authenticate a manifest that declares its own replacement hashes.\nReject mixed, stale, substituted or over-limit distributions before use. This\nasset binding does not protect a client whose executable bundle or publisher\ntrust has itself been replaced.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-3","title":"Language and direction","html":"<h2 id=\"language-and-direction\">Language and direction</h2>\n<p>The initial v1 locale set is English (<code>en</code>) and one Arabic (<code>ar</code>) locale for\nRTL testing, without regional variants. The locale architecture supports\nadditional reviewed languages. Human-facing landing, client, account,\noperator, help/manual/search, support, validation, error, accessibility,\nnotice and communication text uses maintained catalogs. CLI/SDK/API/MCP human\ndescriptions share that vocabulary; machine identifiers, structured error\ncodes, JSON/schema keys, commands, signatures and example code remain stable.</p>\n<p>Locale handling includes plural rules, dates, time zones, numbers and currency,\ntext expansion, logical layout, direction-aware controls and bidi isolation\nfor identifiers, URLs and code. Verify keyboard, screen-reader, browser and\ndevice journeys in LTR and RTL. Arabic uses actual translated content;\npseudolocalization, untranslated fallback and catalog presence do not establish\nlanguage quality or complete journeys. Review translation meaning and technical\naccuracy, track missing/stale text against its source digest and record explicit\nfallback behavior. Vendor-policy language coverage is verified independently\nof application translations.</p>\n<p>Language and light/dark/system theme controls are available before\nauthentication on landing and sign-in journeys as well as relevant client,\naccount and operator pages. Each field resolves an explicit device/session\nchoice first, then an actual active profile preference where supported, then\nthe browser&#x27;s ordered supported language preferences or system theme. Clearing\na device override follows profile/browser defaults; an explicit <code>system</code>\nselection follows the browser even when a profile preference exists. Persist\nonly explicit device choices, never resolved defaults or profile overlays.\nLogout/account switching removes the old profile overlay without copying it\nto anonymous storage or replacing in-progress forms/configuration.</p>\n<p>Language and region are separate. A browser region is a display hint, not\ncountry of origin, nationality, billing address, time zone or authorization.\nAn already available trusted coarse-country signal can supply a regional\nfallback when no browser region exists; no country-to-language mapping is\nassumed. Missing signals use safe defaults. No precise-location prompt, new\nIP-sharing provider, blocking lookup or disruptive late default switch is\nrequired. Keep explicit choices visible and reversible.</p>\n<p>Validate blocked/failing storage, unsupported/multiple browser locales, profile\nand anonymous precedence, reload/account switch, system changes and slow first\nload/theme flash. Native semantic controls support keyboard, screen readers and\nbrowser text-to-speech; no proprietary TTS widget is implied. Check contrast,\nzoom/reflow, forced colors, high contrast and reduced motion against WCAG 2.2 AA\nas a validation reference, without claiming certification.</p>\n<p>Locale catalogs and their manifest use explicitly reviewed initial-v1 contracts.\nConsumers bind the selected schema/content hashes, enforce canonical local\npaths and byte limits, unique locales/IDs, source/translation relationships,\nfile hashes and current source digests. No locale path creates a remote fetch,\nprivate configuration lookup or telemetry permission. Schema validation alone\ncannot establish cross-file integrity or completeness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"browser-services-and-go-live-provisioning\">Browser services and go-live provisioning</h2>\n<p><a href=\"/explore#doc=release&amp;part=part-2&amp;anchor=product-go-live-and-commercialization-service-provisioning\">RELEASE.md</a>\nis the single operator procedure for product go-live, commercialization,\npublisher accounts and applicable service provisioning. Separate CircularQuay\nprojects, approved owners, environments, entitlements, protected references and\nindependent readback cover selected diagnostics, consent/privacy, advertising,\nanalytics, hosting/identity, support/email/feedback and status/discovery services.\nSource hooks, configuration, deployment, activation and entitlement are distinct\nstates; an optional service can be not applicable with an explicit rationale.</p>\n<p>Optional collection defaults off until its purpose, authority/consent, data\nflow and surface suitability are qualified. Public landing approval does not\nmake advertising or analytics appropriate on configuration/account/operator\nsurfaces. Required security audit remains independently mandatory. No private\nconfiguration, keys, credentials, source selectors, protected references, raw\nerrors or help/search queries enter vendor events, URLs or attachments.\nQualify exact CSP origins, retention/deletion, withdrawal, safe failure and\nrelease/source-map/sampling settings on the actual supported platforms. Reused\nintegration patterns do not transfer another product&#x27;s tenant credentials,\nconsent records, service approval or hosting entitlement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-and-remaining-work\">Evidence and remaining work</h2>\n<p>Actual outcomes are machine-readable audit reports and <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-qualification&amp;part=part-1\">qualification receipts</a>, not checkmarks added to this plan. Current live-account, native-host, client and publication status is recorded in <code>RELEASE.md</code>. Claims must stay narrower than the observed evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"agent-packaging-and-documentation-acceptance\">Agent packaging and documentation acceptance</h2>\n<p>The portable cq skill, skill UI metadata and client-plugin instructions share\none capability vocabulary with CLI, SDK and optional MCP. Package only contained,\nversioned references and verified runtime connections; no manifest or skill can\ncreate authority. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-agent-integrations&amp;part=part-1\">Agent integration</a> defines installation,\nreload/removal, compatibility, disclosure and client qualification.</p>\n<p>Architecture, user/admin, provider/identity, CLI/CI/MCP, enterprise, telemetry,\nmanaged-service and release guides describe this same complete v1 end state.\nSchema-backed examples remain executable declarations; integration acceptance\nrequirements cannot be inserted as unsupported fields. Revision-scoped status,\nopen gates and publication receipts stay in qualification/handoff records.</p>\n"}]};
